<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=rubber">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rubber</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rubber is an elastic material produced from the latex of certain tropical trees, which became an important industrial commodity in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries with the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobiles</a> and electrical industries. Colonial powers established plantations and forced-labour regimes to produce it. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> list rubber from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> among the raw materials that colonial economies were organised to supply to French industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rubber.jpg" alt="Rubber" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIAT · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NP_Llanos63_lo_(5853947184).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
